# Dependencies (dev-only; the kernel itself has none). The lockfile IS committed —
# the same convention as nativa-minimal, and dev tooling that resolves differently
# on two machines is a difference nobody asked for.
/vendor/

# Local environment
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Runtime data — the kernel's SQLite files and the things a running site writes.
#
# Ignore the CONTENTS, never the directory. A `/storage/logs/` rule followed by
# `!/storage/logs/.gitkeep` re-includes nothing: git does not descend into an
# ignored directory, so the un-ignore line is unreachable and the skeleton
# silently disappears from the commit. (Measured on this file's first version —
# all five .gitkeep files were ignored.)
/storage/data/*.db
/storage/data/*.db-journal
/storage/data/*.db-wal
/storage/data/*.db-shm
# The engine the Settings panel writes into storage/config/database.json. It
# holds a DSN with a password, so it is runtime state: never committed, and
# never carried into the export (which is built from tracked files).
/storage/config/*.json
# The Blog taxonomy registry is the same kind of thing: a running site writes
# it (BlogServices::taxonomy()->createCategory()), CategoriesManageAction reads
# it back, and TaxonomyRegistryTest snapshots and restores it as SHARED runtime
# state. Upstream does not track its own copy either.
/storage/data/blog-taxonomy.json
/storage/logs/*.log
/storage/cache/*
/storage/sessions/*
/storage/uploads/*
!/storage/data/.gitkeep
!/storage/logs/.gitkeep
!/storage/cache/.gitkeep
!/storage/sessions/.gitkeep
!/storage/uploads/.gitkeep

# Installed module slices (src/Modules/<Name> for <Name> outside Core/Database).
# An installed module is operator state, like a database file: the CATALOGUE
# (modules/<Name>/) is committed, the installed copy is not. Without this
# rule every module:add would stage runtime state into the kernel's own
# history, and the kernel-only claim would rot one committed slice at a time.
# (Gitignore nuance that matters here: `src/Modules/*` ignores the children
# while the re-includes rescue the kernel's own — the inverse, ignoring the
# parent, would make the rescues unreachable.)
src/Modules/*
!src/Modules/Core
!src/Modules/Database
# The kernel's module tooling is committed kernel code too, not operator
# state: bin/cms ships it, so the slice would lose it without this rescue
# (and the move itself would be invisible to git).
!src/Modules/Tooling

# Lekalo runtime cache (regenerated, never committed) — same convention as nativa-minimal.
.lekalo/cache/

# Built frontend bundles (public/dist/<bundle>/ — Vite manifest + hashed
# assets). A build artifact, exactly as in nativa-minimal: the kernel tree
# ships no Frontend/ workspace, so a dist is produced elsewhere and copied
# in (`make assets`), never committed. Without it the admin chrome renders
# unstyled (its CSS comes from the manifest) while every PHP surface still
# answers — see docs/kernel-status.md, Boot 15. The fonts the admin
# stylesheet needs ARE tracked (public/fonts/): they are source, not output.
# NOTE: the trailing `/*` (not a bare dir) is load-bearing — git does not
# descend into an ignored directory, so with a bare `/public/dist/` no
# negation below could ever re-include a frozen bundle (same trap as the
# storage .gitkeep rules above).
/public/dist/*
# Freeze exception (pilot: griffe): the frozen bundle IS committed — a deploy
# without Node serves it byte-identical, and GriffeBuildFreezeTest fails when
# the committed files drift from what `make assets` reproduces. Edit only by
# rebuilding; never by hand.
!/public/dist/griffe/
# Freeze exception (follow-up: mono): same contract as griffe above, guarded
# by MonoBuildFreezeTest.
!/public/dist/mono/
!/public/dist/nativa/
# Freeze exception (admin): the panel stylesheets resolve from this manifest
# and their Gilroy fonts from public/fonts/mark/. Without it /mark renders
# unstyled; same contract as the nativa bundle above.
!/public/dist/moonshine/

# pnpm workspace dependencies (Frontend/packages). The theme SOURCES are
# tracked — they are what `make assets` builds; node_modules and pnpm's
# virtual store are not. The second rule is load-bearing: a package with its
# own dependencies (griffe's `lit`) gets a package-local `node_modules/` full
# of symlinks, and the root-level pattern does not reach into it.
/Frontend/packages/node_modules/
/Frontend/packages/*/node_modules/

# PHPUnit result cache (written on every run, never committed)
.phpunit.result.cache

# Tool caches (php-cs-fixer + Rector write one per run)
.php-cs-fixer.cache
.rector.cache/

# Editor / OS noise
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
*.swp

# Internal agent tooling (never committed, never pushed to GitHub) — with ONE
# exception, decided 2026-10-16: /.ai-factory/patches/ IS committed.
#
# Why the exception: docs/clanky/2026-10-16-posledna-evolucia.md cites a patch
# file by name, and that record was unreadable to anyone but the machine that
# wrote it — the same failure as `evolutions/` being empty, at a scale of one
# document. A patch is a post-mortem of a defect that cost time; keeping it out
# of the repository means the next occurrence is re-diagnosed from scratch.
#
# The other three stay ignored, and the listing is by SUBDIRECTORY rather than
# `/.ai-factory/` plus a negation, because git does not descend into an ignored
# directory: a `!` pattern inside one never matches. So `/.ai-factory/` would
# have kept every patch invisible no matter what followed it.
/.agents/
/.ai-factory.json
/.ai-factory/PLAN.md
/.ai-factory/plans/
/.ai-factory/evolutions/
/.ai-factory/skill-context/
# OpenSpec: the canonical specs are history, a change is not. This is the third
# state of one rule, so all three are written down — the two earlier ones read as
# contradictions on their own, and a reader should not have to date the commits
# to know which one holds.
#
#   2026-10-14  `/openspec/` ignored ("internal tooling too, they stay local")
#   2026-10-27  the whole tree tracked: the specs are the surface the work is
#               JUDGED against, and a clone without them made
#               `ScenarioTestBindingContractTest` SKIP in CI — a check that
#               protects one machine is not a gate, and `.ai-factory/config.yaml`
#               pointed `paths.plans` at a directory a second developer did not
#               have
#   2026-10-27  split to what is below: `specs/` and `config.yaml` are the
#               shared truth, `changes/` is the working plan again
#
# Why a change stays local: a proposal is how the work is organised while it is
# in flight, and the archived ones are `.ai-factory/plans/` migrated verbatim —
# `changes/archive/2026-10-12-settings-theme-switch/proposal.md` still opens
# with the raw request it came from ("super > funguje nam v settings zmena tem
# ?") and carries its own "do not edit, history only" header. Those originals
# under `.ai-factory/plans/` are ignored above, so tracking the copies would make
# this rule the only difference between two copies of one text. What a clone
# needs is the requirement and the test that holds it, and that is `specs/` —
# which is also why the binding gate reads exactly that: a delta is a draft, and
# its scenarios are counted when the change lands in `specs/`.
/openspec/changes/
# Deployable build output (bin/build/build-dist.sh). An artifact, exactly like
# /public/dist/ above: produced by a script, never committed. A trailing
# comment on the pattern line does NOT work here — gitignore has no comment
# syntax after a pattern, so the parentheses became part of it and the rule
# silently matched nothing.
/dist/
# Release artifacts, one directory per build
# (`bin/build/build-dist.sh --to releases/<UTC-date>`). Same rationale as
# /dist/: the directory is what gets uploaded and what a rollback points at,
# not source. Immutability is the rollback story, so it is kept on disk.
/releases/
# A checkout of the app the gallery sync can read instead of this project
# (`NATIVA_APP=/path/to/checkout`). Never committed: the gallery `src/` is
# the copy, and two copies in git is the drift that `sync:check` catches.
/.upstream/

# The adopted pre-rename database backup is operator state, not code.
/storage/data/*.adopted-backup

# The installed target-adapter store. `lekalo adapter install` writes it from
# `adapters/php-nativa`; the package bytes live in the repository, the pinned
# inventory is custody. Re-installing after a byte change is the update path.
/.lekalo/adapters/
